xnu 0352: a kernel abort leaves the panel to the UI, and parks its numbers in the ring

Measured 2026-11-18 on a boot that kept running: `KABORT esr 0x96000047 far
0xfffffe1f15edc000 pc 0xfffffe007c2dfc98` on the panel, and from that moment the
console owned the screen -- every later log line and status row over the UI,
the lock screen answering no touch, and only a retried boot gave a clean panel.

The cause is the order in handle_kernel_abort(): the six register rows and
mondrian_early_panic_begin() run before the handler decides anything, and the
`vm_fault()` below can resolve the fault, after which the thread returns and the
boot carries on -- with the panel still taken. mondrian_early_panic_begin() has
no other caller, so a fault the machine survives is the only case it serves, and
that case is exactly the one that should not paint.

The numbers go to the ring instead, which needs no panel. A fault that really
does panic still paints: panic_with_thread_kernel_state() calls
mondrian_early_fault(), which sets its own forced flag and draws the panic rows
with the full register dump and, with keepsyms=1, the symbol.

Where the observed fault is: its lr (0xfffffe0007584a68) resolves to
_mapping_free_prime_internal in the pmap pv-free path -- the same path this board
is already documented to panic in when the panel alias is torn out (0351) -- so
it is a pmap fault being reported, not a reason to give it the screen.

--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -1524,45 +1524,21 @@
 	bool recover = find_copyio_recovery_entry(state) != 0;
 #if defined(MONDRIAN)
 	/*
-	 * A fault under copyin/copyout is recovered below and happens on any boot
-	 * (a page of the caller's buffer not resident yet): painting its six
-	 * register rows put text on top of a live UI, several times a second,
-	 * and said nothing that the ring did not. Only a fault that is about to
-	 * panic is worth the screen -- and then the console is turned on for it,
-	 * because a boot that has not panicked yet has not painted anything.
+	 * This runs before the handler knows whether the fault is fatal: the
+	 * `vm_fault` below can fix it and this thread carries on. Painting the
+	 * registers here therefore took the panel from the compositor for a fault
+	 * the machine survived -- measured 2026-11-18, `KABORT esr 0x96000047` on
+	 * the glass of a boot that kept running, the console on from that moment
+	 * and every later log line over the UI, with the lock screen answering no
+	 * touch. The numbers go to the ring instead, which needs no panel, and a
+	 * fault that really does panic paints through mondrian_early_fault() in
+	 * panic_with_thread_kernel_state().
 	 */
-	if (!recover) {
-		extern void mondrian_early_reg(const char *name, uint64_t value);
-		extern void mondrian_early_panic_begin(void);
-		extern void mondrian_early_status(const char *s);
-		extern const char *mondrian_panic_format;
-		uint64_t k_cpsr = get_saved_state_cpsr(state);
-
-		mondrian_early_panic_begin();
-
-		mondrian_early_reg("KABORT esr", esr);
-		mondrian_early_reg("KABORT far", fault_addr);
-		mondrian_early_reg("KABORT pc", get_saved_state_pc(state));
-		mondrian_early_reg("KABORT lr", saved_state64(state)->lr);
-		mondrian_early_reg("KABORT cpsr", k_cpsr);
-		mondrian_early_reg("KABORT x0", saved_state64(state)->x[0]);
-		if (mondrian_panic_format != NULL) {
-			char hud[120];
-			int n = 0;
-
-			hud[n++] = 'P';
-			hud[n++] = ':';
-			hud[n++] = ' ';
-			for (int i = 0; mondrian_panic_format[i] != 0 &&
-			    n < (int)sizeof(hud) - 1; i++) {
-				char c = mondrian_panic_format[i];
-
-				hud[n++] = (c == '\n' || c == '\r' || c == '\t') ? ' ' : c;
-			}
-			hud[n] = 0;
-			mondrian_early_status(hud);
-		}
-	}
+	kprintf("MONDRIAN KABORT: %s esr 0x%x far 0x%llx pc 0x%llx lr 0x%llx"
+	    " cpsr 0x%x x0 0x%llx\n", recover ? "recovered" : "unrecovered",
+	    esr, (uint64_t)fault_addr, get_saved_state_pc(state),
+	    saved_state64(state)->lr, get_saved_state_cpsr(state),
+	    saved_state64(state)->x[0]);
 #endif
 
